# Keep the working tree byte-identical on every platform.
#
# Without this, git's Windows default (`core.autocrlf=true`) checks the tree out
# with CRLF, and two mechanisms this repo relies on stop working — neither of
# them loudly:
#
#   · `npm run format:check` fails on EVERY file. Prettier defaults to
#     `endOfLine: "lf"`, so a stock Windows clone reports ~88 files as
#     misformatted while being byte-identical to Prettier's output apart from
#     the line endings. Measured: `package.json` normalised to LF equals
#     `prettier package.json` exactly.
#
#   · every multi-line pattern in `tools/guards.mjs` matches ZERO times, so
#     `npm run verify:guards` reports PATTERN DRIFT instead of proving the
#     controls load-bearing — the mechanism that exists to catch a check which
#     silently measures nothing, silently measuring nothing.
#
# `tools/guard-match.mjs` makes the guard runner tolerate CRLF regardless, so a
# contributor who has already cloned is not stuck. This file is the actual fix.
#
# NOTE, because the usual advice does not apply here: `git add --renormalize .`
# does NOTHING in this repo. That command fixes a repository whose COMMITTED
# content is CRLF. This one was authored on Linux, so the committed content is
# already LF — measured with `git ls-files --eol`, which reports `i/lf` for every
# tracked file. There is nothing to renormalise and the commit would be empty.
#
# What is stale in an older clone is the WORKING TREE, written as CRLF before
# this file existed. Refreshing it is not a commit at all — git reports no change
# afterwards, because only the line endings differed:
#
#     git status                       # confirm a clean tree first
#     git ls-files -z | xargs -0 rm -f
#     git checkout -- .
#
# `git checkout-index -a -f` looks like it should do this and does not: git skips
# files it considers up to date via the stat cache, so they must actually be
# removed first.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
